WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.042 --> 00:00:03.378
Out of the millions of fungal species 
in the world

00:00:03.378 --> 00:00:05.755
only a few hundred can 
make people sick.

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:08.466
Coccidioides is one of them.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.011
Its spores can sometimes cause

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:12.971
pretty serious infections in humans.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:16.391
But that's not the weirdest thing 
about this fungus.

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:18.893
Most fungi prefer dark, damp

00:00:18.893 --> 00:00:21.021
and relatively cool conditions...

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:24.315
but this one has found a way 
to live in the desert.

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:27.944
And while most fungi eat dead 
or decaying material...

00:00:28.778 --> 00:00:32.198
cocci doesn't always wait 
for an animal to die...

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:37.912
before it starts digesting them 
from the inside out.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.416
Drama aside, this fungus has 
existed for millennia

00:00:41.416 --> 00:00:42.959
and yet there's still so many things

00:00:42.959 --> 00:00:44.502
we don't understand about it.

00:00:44.502 --> 00:00:47.380
Like exactly where it is 
at any given time...

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:50.050
or how it spreads in the soil...

00:00:50.842 --> 00:00:53.344
because it is spreading.

00:00:53.344 --> 00:00:55.305
And we can only guess why.

00:01:00.185 --> 00:01:02.896
The infection caused by cocci goes by many names.

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:04.522
Coccidioidomycosis.

00:01:04.522 --> 00:01:05.607
Desert Rheumatism.

00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:07.484
San Joaquin Valley Fever.

00:01:07.484 --> 00:01:09.319
Or just Valley Fever.

00:01:09.944 --> 00:01:12.030
Its symptoms are similar 
to that of the flu

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.241
except that general achey-ness, fever
fatigue, and more

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:16.868
can last for over a month.

00:01:17.035 --> 00:01:19.037
Others develop fungal pneumonia

00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:21.414
but some can develop something far worse

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:24.959
like chronic lung disease, skin absences 
or meningitis 

00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:27.253
which can be fatal.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.380
Fortunately, only about four in ten people

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.426
ever develop symptoms at all 
and outbreaks are pretty rare.

00:01:33.802 --> 00:01:37.013
Right now, 97% of 
US Valley Fever cases

00:01:37.013 --> 00:01:39.265
occur in Arizona or California.

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:40.558
But...

00:01:40.558 --> 00:01:41.810
Not all states report.

00:01:41.976 --> 00:01:44.604
So Texas is one of those areas that...

00:01:45.146 --> 00:01:48.191
you know, they just don't 
report the disease nationally

00:01:48.191 --> 00:01:50.527
in spite of being probably a hotspot.

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:54.697
In 2019 states officially reported

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:57.450
20,000 cases of Valley Fever to the CDC.

00:01:58.034 --> 00:01:59.953
Some researchers think that actual number

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:03.289
is closer to 350,000 cases annually.

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:06.209
If you account for underreporting
and misdiagnoses.

00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:07.794
Where it is reported...

00:02:07.836 --> 00:02:10.171
cases are rising year after year...

00:02:10.213 --> 00:02:13.883
with California reporting a 159% increase

00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:15.927
from 2013 to 2019.

00:02:16.594 --> 00:02:18.638
And it's being found in more places.

00:02:18.888 --> 00:02:22.100
Cocci is typically found in the soil
in the southwestern US

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.519
parts of Mexico, Central, 
and South America.

00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:28.565
But in 2014, researchers found it 
in Washington State.

00:02:29.774 --> 00:02:33.069
It's likely the fungus itself is already 
lurking in the soil

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:35.155
in states outside of this range.

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:37.073
And if that's not unsettling enough

00:02:37.073 --> 00:02:40.243
we're not really sure 
how it's moving...

00:02:40.743 --> 00:02:43.830
or where exactly it's going next.

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:48.251
Scientists think there might be 
three separate systems

00:02:48.251 --> 00:02:50.503
driving the spread of this mysterious fungus.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:54.215
Let's start with some bizarre features 
of cocci itself.

00:02:55.383 --> 00:02:56.551
It has two forms:

00:02:56.551 --> 00:02:59.262
An environmental form that lives 
in the desert soil.

00:02:59.304 --> 00:03:03.016
So the fungus in the dirt grows as a mold.

00:03:03.224 --> 00:03:04.517
Sort of not so different

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:07.061
from what you see on bread 
when it gets old.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:10.899
But like, we're talking lightweight, 
microscopic mold

00:03:10.899 --> 00:03:15.361
that grows very slowly in soil, 
making it hard to track.

00:03:15.904 --> 00:03:17.071
When the soil is disturbed

00:03:17.071 --> 00:03:19.073
like when dust storms sweep 
through the west

00:03:19.073 --> 00:03:21.701
or when construction workers begin 
digging a foundation...

00:03:21.993 --> 00:03:24.245
cocci releases spores into the air

00:03:24.245 --> 00:03:26.372
where unsuspecting mammals 
breathe them in.

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:28.917
Once inhaled, it settles into the lungs

00:03:28.917 --> 00:03:32.003
where those spores somehow 
morph into a new form...

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:35.632
a parasitic form that multiplies in your body

00:03:35.632 --> 00:03:38.927
and grows to a size much larger 
than its environmental form.

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:40.803
Through a series of cues that we actually don't

00:03:40.803 --> 00:03:43.890
really understand all that well, 
it can be like 100 microns.

00:03:43.932 --> 00:03:45.266
You can see the lesions

00:03:45.266 --> 00:03:47.685
in infected lung tissue 
with the naked eye.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.437
Some people who inhale these spores

00:03:49.437 --> 00:03:51.105
get the symptoms of Valley Fever.

00:03:51.105 --> 00:03:55.276
But sometimes, somehow, 
and this is what's really weird...

00:03:55.777 --> 00:03:58.613
it can go dormant in your lungs.

00:03:58.821 --> 00:04:00.698
You actually develop

00:04:00.698 --> 00:04:03.284
residency of that fungus somewhere 
in your body.

00:04:03.326 --> 00:04:05.411
Being dormant is a great strategy, right?

00:04:05.453 --> 00:04:07.455
You're not using many resources.

00:04:07.997 --> 00:04:11.125
The host isn't recognizing you 
because you're not actively

00:04:11.125 --> 00:04:13.836
growing and irritating the host.

00:04:14.003 --> 00:04:15.713
Yeah. Then you just bide your time.

00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:18.883
Cocci isn't picky about what kind 
of lungs it's settling in.

00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:21.427
Infections have been found in 
almost any type of mammal

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:24.097
from humans to dogs 
to dolphins.

00:04:24.305 --> 00:04:28.518
Which brings us to the second thing
that’s possibly helping this fungus spread:

00:04:28.518 --> 00:04:32.730
Desert rodents and something called 
the Endozoan Hypothesis.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.276
I hope you're ready to talk about 
dehydrated rats.

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:38.319
It works like this.

00:04:39.195 --> 00:04:41.864
A kangaroo rat or some other 
desert rodent

00:04:41.864 --> 00:04:43.908
inhales cocci while digging its burrow.

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:46.661
The cocci either lays dormant 
in their lungs

00:04:46.661 --> 00:04:48.121
or the infection kills them.

00:04:48.496 --> 00:04:49.205
At that point

00:04:49.205 --> 00:04:52.166
the fungus is the first on the scene
to devour the dead animal.

00:04:52.583 --> 00:04:53.501
And by devour

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:56.921
I mean digest its dead body 
until there's nothing left.

00:04:57.088 --> 00:04:59.674
Fun fact this is what cocci 
is doing to people

00:04:59.674 --> 00:05:01.634
with severe valley fever.

00:05:02.176 --> 00:05:03.678
While they're alive.

00:05:03.678 --> 00:05:07.390
Their spinal column is being 
digested by this organism.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:09.475
And it just freaks me out.

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:11.477
Either way, it's the first one on the scene.

00:05:11.561 --> 00:05:13.062
Not even you're the best competitor

00:05:13.062 --> 00:05:14.605
or you're the best at doing anything.

00:05:14.605 --> 00:05:16.107
It’s just, you're there first.

00:05:16.107 --> 00:05:18.901
And so maybe that's cocci’s strategy.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.279
When the rodent dies, 
cocci is released

00:05:21.279 --> 00:05:24.949
back into the soil and morphs back 
into its environmental form

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:28.036
where it waits to be kicked up 
and inhaled by some other host.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:31.456
If this is the case, then 
the more desert rodents

00:05:31.456 --> 00:05:34.584
living and dying, the more likely
cocci can move through the soil.

00:05:35.585 --> 00:05:37.879
Which brings us to our third related factor:

00:05:38.379 --> 00:05:39.839
Extreme weather.

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:42.133
Isn’t is always?

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:45.094
Heavy rains have been unleashing
on the desert

00:05:45.094 --> 00:05:46.721
resulting in booms of vegetation.

00:05:47.138 --> 00:05:49.599
The surplus food leads to 
a surplus of rodents...

00:05:49.724 --> 00:05:51.893
plenty of hosts to breathe in the fungus.

00:05:52.435 --> 00:05:54.479
But the desert ultimately returns...

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:58.024
to a desert and heavy drought 
wipes out more and more

00:05:58.024 --> 00:05:59.692
of these infected rodents.

00:05:59.984 --> 00:06:02.987
When they die, cocci spread
right back into the soil

00:06:02.987 --> 00:06:04.530
and the system starts again.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:09.285
But we still don't know if this is 
actually even happening...

00:06:09.327 --> 00:06:12.246
because of how little we understand 
about cocci itself.

00:06:12.747 --> 00:06:15.625
Proving the Endozoan Hypothesis 
would be a colossal effort

00:06:15.625 --> 00:06:18.378
that involves strapping trackers 
to a bunch of desert rodents.

00:06:18.753 --> 00:06:21.631
And finding cocci in the soil at all 
has proven difficult.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.759
The current tests don't always 
detect it in the soil...

00:06:24.967 --> 00:06:26.052
even when it's there.

00:06:27.261 --> 00:06:30.848
Ultimately, cocci’s spread spread could be 
thanks to all of these factors combined

00:06:30.848 --> 00:06:33.309
but there are still tons of unresolved questions.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.728
What we do know 
is that the US

00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:39.899
is getting warmer and that on its own 
could expand cocci’s range

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:41.484
potentially making more people sick.

00:06:42.026 --> 00:06:43.903
One study estimates that cocci’s range

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:46.114
could more than double by 2095...

00:06:46.406 --> 00:06:48.866
due to increasing arid conditions 
across the US.

00:06:49.534 --> 00:06:52.453
Pair all of this with rising rates 
of construction in the desert...

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:55.331
and population booms in cities like Phoenix.

00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:58.000
And it's no surprise that Valley Fever cases

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.460
are on the rise.

00:07:01.379 --> 00:07:05.091
Researchers have another area of study 
right now: Prevention.

00:07:05.466 --> 00:07:09.345
We have identified a vaccine candidate...

00:07:09.512 --> 00:07:14.642
that is both safe and protective, 
very protective to mice.

00:07:14.642 --> 00:07:18.396
And now we've shown that in 
experimentally infected dogs...

00:07:18.855 --> 00:07:20.148
it protects them as well.

00:07:20.148 --> 00:07:23.359
And that vaccine is on its way.

00:07:23.526 --> 00:07:26.446
But listen, despite my 
seemingly best efforts

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:28.906
this piece doesn't really exist to scare you

00:07:28.906 --> 00:07:32.326
or to get you to hyper focus on 
one specific freaky infection.

00:07:32.994 --> 00:07:35.663
This single fungus is one of 
a million examples

00:07:35.663 --> 00:07:38.875
of how our world is changing 
in small and mysterious ways...

00:07:39.375 --> 00:07:41.502
and how difficult it can sometimes be

00:07:41.502 --> 00:07:43.045
for science to keep up.

